{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"inaccurate-genetic-asianlion","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Observation\nIn `sweep (windows)` diag run 44593555306 (throwaway branch `w1-diag-opcert-acl`, carrying the plump-smelly-indianabat, undamaging-ashamed-wasp and flowered-nonvalued-mink fixes), `test_opcert_key_copy_leak_tortoise.py::test_signer_test_modules_leave_no_key_copy_in_the_temp_dir` failed. The nested run passed. The sandbox TMPDIR it pointed at still held 13 or more `rebar-opcert-ws-*` job workspaces. 323 of the remaining files were read-only, all under `<ws>\\.git\\objects\\..`.\n\n## Cause\n`src/rebar/opcert_service/workspace.py::discard` removes a job workspace with `shutil.rmtree(root, ignore_errors=True)`. Git writes loose objects and packs read-only. On Windows, deleting a read-only file fails with PermissionError. `ignore_errors=True` swallows that, so every op-cert job on Windows leaves its workspace (a clone of the ticket store) behind in the host temp dir. POSIX deletes read-only files in a writable dir, so the leak is Windows-only.\n\n## Approach\nIn `workspace.discard`, pass an error handler to `shutil.rmtree`. On a failed removal it clears the read-only attribute with `os.chmod(path, stat.S_IWRITE)` and retries the same operation once. Any error that remains is still swallowed, so cleanup stays best-effort and never raises into the job. The handler uses `onexc=` on Python 3.12+ and `onerror=` on 3.11 (requires-python >= 3.11), selected by `sys.version_info`. No config key, env var or flag. Portable: the chmod-and-retry path only runs after a failure, so POSIX behaviour is unchanged. `os.chmod(S_IWRITE)` is the documented way to clear the Windows read-only attribute.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/opcert_service/workspace.py` — the `discard` handler.\n- `tests/unit/test_opcert_workspace_discard_readonly.py` (new) — tests below.\n\n## Testing\n- New test (all platforms): build a tree with a read-only file inside a read-only subdirectory. Patch `os.unlink`/`os.remove` (as rmtree calls them) to raise PermissionError while the target lacks S_IWRITE, which reproduces Windows semantics on POSIX. `discard` removes the whole tree.\n- New test: a removal that still fails after the retry does not raise from `discard`.\n- After merge: the leak test passes in a `sweep (windows)` run on main.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `workspace.discard` removes a workspace containing read-only files on Windows semantics, proven by a test that reproduces the read-only refusal, and never raises.\n- [ ] `test_signer_test_modules_leave_no_key_copy_in_the_temp_dir` passes in a `sweep (windows)` run on main.","id":"387c-d59c-7854-40b2","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["windows","portability","signing"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Windows: op-cert job workspaces leak in the temp dir because rmtree cannot delete read-only git objects"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1795115025331783185,"uuid":"f395adf1-97b3-4aa4-a1e2-27bf9f124e0b"}